::GEO_U03_UA_Q01::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U03_UA_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.2<br><br>Question:<br>A triangle has angles 42&deg; and 71&deg;. What is the third angle? {
=A. 67&deg;#42 + 71 = 113, and 180 - 113 = 67.
~B. 57&deg;#This does not complete the angle sum.
~C. 77&deg;#This makes the total too large.
~D. 113&deg;#This is the sum of the two given angles.
}

::GEO_U03_UA_Q02::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U03_UA_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.1<br><br>Question:<br>Which criterion proves congruence from three side pairs? {
~A. SAS#SAS uses two sides and an included angle.
=B. SSS#SSS uses three corresponding side pairs.
~C. ASA#ASA uses two angles and the included side.
~D. AAA#AAA does not prove congruence.
}

::GEO_U03_UA_Q03::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U03_UA_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.3<br><br>Question:<br>Why can valid congruence criteria connect to rigid transformations? {
~A. They change side lengths.#Rigid transformations preserve side lengths.
~B. They rely only on appearance.#Appearance is not proof.
=C. They give enough evidence to map one triangle exactly onto another.#Valid criteria justify exact matching by rigid motion.
~D. They prove only similarity.#Congruence criteria prove congruence, not only similarity.
}

::GEO_U03_UA_Q04::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U03_UA_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.RP.2<br><br>Question:<br>In a parallelogram, opposite sides are what? {
~A. Always perpendicular#Perpendicular sides are not guaranteed.
~B. Always unequal#Opposite sides have a congruence relationship.
~C. Unrelated#They are related by parallelogram properties.
=D. Congruent#Opposite sides of a parallelogram are congruent.
}

::GEO_U03_UA_Q05::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U03_UA_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.RP.3<br><br>Question:<br>In a trapezoid, the parallel sides are called what? {
=A. Bases#The parallel sides are the bases.
~B. Legs#Legs are non-parallel sides.
~C. Diagonals#Diagonals connect nonadjacent vertices.
~D. Vertex angles#Vertex angle is triangle language.
}

::GEO_U03_UA_Q06::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U03_UA_Q06<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.2<br><br>Question:<br>A triangle has angles 42&deg; and 71&deg;. What is the third angle? Choose the most precise answer. {
